  ('lib-bdccc7259ec4e0b34c8a09f4', 'Problems of a Coordinator · Time changes go through you', '10:00 a.m. The bride is ready for prep shots. The photographer isn''t coming until 11. ⤵
He moved his call time a week ago. He told the couple. Nobody told you.

He wasn''t wrong to ask for a later call. He asked the wrong person, in a chat you weren''t in, and the couple said ''sige po'' without knowing the hair team, the car and the church time all hang on that hour.

Now your run-of-show is a guess, and you find out at the worst possible time: on the day.

On Setnayan, time changes go through the coordinator. A supplier proposes a new time, and you decide: approve it, or keep the old one. When you approve, the schedule changes for everyone who reads it, not just the person who asked.

One line to add to every supplier brief, starting this week: ''All time changes go through me, po, even small ones.'' Suppliers usually appreciate it. It protects their call time too.

Coordinators: how do you stop the quiet time changes?

