  ('lib-13e115bec43136d8f87f738f', 'Problems of a Coordinator · Scan the pass, not the list', '5:30 p.m. Tita Cora at the door: ''Nasaan yung listahan? May ballpen ka?'' ⤵
Twelve printed pages. Three Dela Cruzes. A queue to the parking lot.

She means well, and she''s doing a hard job with the wrong tool. Paper lists are slow at the door, and they can''t tell you anything useful once the doors are open: who''s inside, which tables are full, how many plates the caterer should be plating right now.

On Setnayan, every guest has their own QR pass, the same one they used to reply. Your door crew scans it, and arrivals, +1s and tables update live. The count at the door is measured against the guests who said yes, so when the caterer asks how many are in, you have a real number, not ''mga 150 siguro.'' The kitchen can plate for the room that''s actually there.

Give Tita a job she''ll love instead: greeting people by name.

