  ('lib-2dbf00894db0c1ba54653c86', 'Problems of a Coordinator · A debut program that ends on time', '54 names before the cake is cut. ⤵
The dances aren''t what run a debut two hours late. The gaps are.

The time goes on calling a rose from the far table, hunting for the next name, waiting for a song change. Three things help: keep the next three roses standing by the floor, group the candles by family, and give the emcee one written order.

On Setnayan, a debut''s 18 roses, 18 candles and 18 treasures sit in the run of show, in order, so you, the emcee and the host follow the same list. If the program runs late, shift everything: move one moment and every supplier''s time moves with it. And when a supplier wants to change a time, they propose it and you decide.

One more habit: agree the song cut for the roses with the band or DJ before the day. A short, fixed cut per rose does more for the clock than any reminder on the mic.

