  ('lib-f8df07f984dd31ed703ec6e0', 'The Problem People Didn''t Know Existed · Be in every supplier chat', 'It''s 6 a.m. at the venue. The lights supplier arrives and asks, "Sino po yung coordinator?" ⤵
It''s the first time you''ve ever spoken.

It happens more than couples think. Suppliers are booked months apart, often before the coordinator is. The couple talks to each one in a separate chat. By the time the coordinator comes in, the agreements already exist, and the coordinator knows them only second-hand: a forwarded screenshot, a voice message, "sabi ni ate kasama na daw yung setup."

So on the day, the person running the program is the one person who never heard the details from the supplier directly. Every gap shows up at the worst time: the call time nobody confirmed, the extra hour the band thought was included.

On Setnayan, a booked coordinator is in every supplier chat on the event. Not a summary of it: the chat itself, with the quotes and changes as cards. And when a supplier wants a different time, they propose it and the coordinator decides.

You can meet them at 6 a.m. and already know what they agreed to.

