  ('lib-3cf44810037fb4ad4fb69821', 'The Problem People Didn''t Know Existed · Count yeses, ask the maybes', '''Baka po, depende sa schedule.'' You count them in, just in case. ⤵
Then you do it thirty more times.

Most hosts give the caterer one number. But that number is usually yeses plus maybes plus the +1s nobody named, rounded up. Thirty maybes at a buffet is thirty plates, thirty chairs and maybe three tables, and on the day, many of those maybes don''t come.

The fix starts with keeping the maybes separate. A yes is a plate. A maybe is a question you still need to ask.

On Setnayan, your live guest count keeps them apart: attending, pending, maybe and declined, with +1 seats counted, current every time you open it. You see exactly how many maybes you''re carrying, and who they are, so you can ask them a week before the caterer''s deadline. Meal counts come from the RSVPs, ready for the caterer, so the number you send is the number your guests gave.

