  ('lib-bb6af2871b649b344b6d1f5e', 'The Problem People Didn''t Know Existed · Sent and received, both recorded', '''Nag-send na po ako!'' with a screenshot. The supplier checks the bank. Nothing yet. ⤵
Both of them are telling the truth.

A screenshot proves a transfer was started. It doesn''t prove it arrived: transfers can be delayed, sent to the wrong account, or reversed. So the host believes they''ve paid, the supplier believes they haven''t, and nobody is lying. Weeks later, when the balance is due, the two records disagree, and the argument starts from scratch.

The fix is to treat ''sent'' and ''received'' as two separate steps, each confirmed by the person who can see it.

On Setnayan, the host logs each payment with its receipt, and the supplier marks it received or not received. Both records are kept, side by side, so a missing payment is visible the day it goes missing, not the week of the event. Nobody has to argue from memory, and nobody has to feel accused for asking.

