  ('lib-bf4e31ad90964ceb328d7e86', 'Problems of a Coordinator · Move one moment, not twelve chats', '3 p.m. The rain starts early. The garden ceremony moves inside. ⤵
Now every time after it moves too, and twelve people need to know.

Rain plans aren''t really about tents. They''re about the domino effect: a ceremony moved by forty minutes pushes the cocktail hour, the entrance, dinner and the band''s set. The coordinator ends up retyping the day into twelve chats.

On Setnayan, you shift the schedule once. Move one moment and every supplier''s time moves with it. Booked suppliers see the running order on their desk inside the Event Hub, and guests can open their own link to find their seat and see the running schedule.

What Setnayan can''t do is choose your plan B. Write it in June, with the family and the venue: the indoor room you''ll move to, the covered walkway from the cars, the time you''ll make the call and the first three people who hear it. A plan B that lives only in your head is a plan nobody else can follow.

What''s in your rain kit that clients never think about?

