The food and the venue are rarely what goes wrong at a big office party. The chain of command is. HR booked you. The boss''s secretary has opinions about the raffle. The emcee takes requests from the floor. And every supplier is getting instructions from whoever reached them first.

Two things help most. First, agree in writing who on the client side approves changes: one name. Second, make sure every supplier knows the coordinator owns the program.

On Setnayan, a booked coordinator is in every supplier chat on the event, with every quote and change posted as a card. When a supplier needs a different time, they propose it and the coordinator decides. And if dinner runs late, you shift one moment and every supplier''s time moves with it.

