  ('lib-b878733524b30a51788ea765', 'The Problem People Didn''t Know Existed · Reviews only from real bookings', 'You''re comparing two photographers. One has 140 five-star reviews. ⤵
How many of them were written by people who actually booked?

On most pages there''s no way to know. A review can come from anyone with an account: a friend, a relative, the assistant, the owner''s second profile. Most are written kindly, not dishonestly. But the stars add up the same, and a careful host reading them can''t tell a client''s experience from a cousin''s encouragement.

It cuts the other way too. A supplier with fewer reviews may simply have fewer friends online, not fewer happy clients.

On Setnayan, only a host whose booking with that supplier was completed can review them. The shop''s owner and team, and anyone reviewing themselves, don''t count toward the stars. Guests'' notes are kept separate. And the supplier gets one public reply.

So a star on Setnayan means one thing: someone booked them, and the event happened.

Have you ever believed a review you later doubted?

