  ('lib-be3cfde05fa7d7751072d182', 'The Problem People Didn''t Know Existed · Every quote needs a valid-until date', 'The quote you said yes to may have expired a week ago. ⤵
And neither of you would know.

Here''s how it usually goes. A supplier sends rates in a chat in June. The couple says ''Sige po, we''ll take it'' in August. The supplier''s costs went up in July. Both of them think the June price still stands, until the first bill.

Nobody lied. The quote just never said how long it was good for, so each side remembered it the way that made sense to them.

A price without a date is half a promise. For suppliers it means honouring old rates you can''t afford, or a hard ''may adjustment po'' conversation. For couples it means a surprise.

On Setnayan, every quote is a card in the chat with a valid-until date: 7, 14 or 30 days, or a date the supplier picks. Both sides see when it ends. And once the couple books, the price is fixed at the supplier''s yes, so a later price change doesn''t touch them.

Suppliers: do your quotes say how long they''re good for?

